{"title":"Ishika's Garden Mystery","gradeSubjectTag":"Class 5 · Science · Photosynthesis","readMinutes":2,"scenes":[{"label":"hook","text":"Ishika was helping her grandmother water the plants on their balcony garden one morning. She noticed the neem tree's leaves were bright green, the tomato plant's leaves were green, even the mint plant's leaves were green. 'Dadi, why are all the leaves green?' she asked."},{"label":"problem","text":"Her grandmother smiled and said, 'That's a good question. But first, tell me—do you think the leaves are just painted green, or is there something inside them doing that?' Ishika looked closely and wondered. She didn't know the answer, and it puzzled her."},{"label":"mathMoment","text":"'Let me show you,' said Dadi. 'Inside each leaf is a special green colour called chlorophyll. The chlorophyll traps sunlight—the energy from the sun—and uses it to make food for the plant. That's why leaves are green. When sunlight comes, the chlorophyll catches it, and the plant grows strong. No sunlight, no chlorophyll working, and the leaf would turn yellow or brown.' Ishika understood: the leaves are green because they are busy catching the sun to feed the whole plant."},{"label":"wrapUp","text":"From that day, whenever Ishika watered the plants, she knew the green leaves were working hard in the sunlight. She even started a small notebook to draw the leaves and write down what she saw. Her grandmother said, 'You are a scientist now, Ishika. You asked, you learned, and now you see the world differently.'"}],"visual":null,"equations":[],"tryTogether":{"question":"You see a plant leaf that has turned yellow and stopped growing. Using what you know about chlorophyll, can you explain why this might have happened?","answer":"The leaf may not be getting enough sunlight, so the chlorophyll cannot trap energy and make food for the plant."},"parentPrompt":"Walk outside together and point to different leaves—ask your child which are greenest and why they think that leaf is healthier."}
